<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Okinawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Okinawa was fought from April to June 1945 between American and Japanese forces on Okinawa, the largest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> on Japanese territory. It killed about 100,000 Japanese soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, more than 12,000 Americans and tens of thousands of Okinawan civilians, and destroyed Shuri Castle and much of the island. Okinawa remained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> administration until 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-okinawa.jpg" alt="Battle of Okinawa" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Staff Sergent Walter F. Kleine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ww2_158.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle, codenamed Operation Iceberg, followed a long campaign of island hopping. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> planned to use Kadena Air Base on Okinawa as a staging point for Operation Downfall, the invasion of the Japanese home islands 550 kilometres away. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> formed the Tenth Army, combining Army and Marine divisions, with its own tactical air force. Against it stood Lieutenant General Mitsuru Ushijima's Thirty-Second Army, a mixed force of regular troops, naval infantry and conscripted local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Okinawans</a>, about 100,000 Japanese troops in all at the start.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The 77th Infantry Division captured the Kerama Islands from 26 March 1945. On 28 March, 394 civilians on Tokashiki island were forced by Japanese soldiers to kill themselves after American troops landed. The main landing took place on 1 April on the Hagushi beaches on the west coast, and the Tenth Army quickly took the Kadena and Yomitan airbases. The 6th Marine Division cleared the north, while two Army divisions turned south and met fierce resistance at fortified outposts guarding the Shuri Line. The Japanese fought from prepared cave positions, and the advance cost many lives on both sides. The land battle lasted about 81 days.</p><p>At sea, Japanese attacks came in waves. Between 1 April and 25 May seven major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamikaze">kamikaze</a> attacks involved more than 1,500 planes. In April the battleship Yamato and nine other warships sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on a suicide sortie, Operation Ten-Go; American carrier aircraft sank Yamato and five other warships before they reached Okinawa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Americans suffered some 48,000 casualties, more than 12,000 of them killed or missing. Lieutenant General Simon Bolivar Buckner Jr., the commander of the Tenth Army, was killed by artillery fire four days before the campaign ended, the highest-ranking American officer killed by enemy fire in the war. Okinawa had a large civilian population, and about 40,000 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Okinawans</a> killed had been drafted or impressed by the Japanese army. The Cornerstone of Peace at Mabuni, erected in 1995, lists the names of all the known dead, civilian and military, Japanese and foreign.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> surrendered soon after, so the planned invasion of the home islands never took place. Some military historians hold that the cost of Okinawa led American planners to seek an alternative in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a>, while others point to the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and Japan's depleted resources.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Okinawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
